WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.565
- [Narrator] This is a Formula
One pit stop in real time.

00:00:03.565 --> 00:00:06.482
(machine whirring)

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.900
Within just two and a half seconds,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.520
all four tires are changed

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.880
and even the car setup was modified.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.250
And to understand how that is possible,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.230
I've animated the entire thing

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.731
so we can take a look
at it in slow motion.

00:00:21.731 --> 00:00:24.570
(upbeat music)

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.270
The first factor determining
the speed of the stop is

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.660
how well the driver manages
getting into the pits.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.750
Every pit lane has a strict speed limit.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.600
On tracks with particularly
tight or short layouts

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.440
like Monaco, the speed is
capped at 60 kilometers an hour,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.000
but on most circuits
it's 80 or about 50 MPH.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.760
In order to lose little time,
the driver tries to break

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.540
as late as possible when
entering the pit lane.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.250
Once past this line,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.280
the driver presses a dedicated
button on the steering wheel

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.563
that locks the car to the speed limit.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.220
Next, the driver turns into the box

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.460
and has to place the car
as precise as possible

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.570
as the entire pit crew is
positioned with pinpoint accuracy.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.970
If the driver overshoots even slightly,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.900
the whole team has to adjust,
stepping forward to compensate

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.883
and wasting valuable time.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.570
To help the driver nail their mark,

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.530
teams use a mechanical arm
with an arrow in the pit box

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.230
and the perfect stop
happens when the front tires

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.783
line up exactly beneath that arrow.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.780
One of the boldest roles in the pit crew

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.270
belongs to the front jack operator.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.300
This person stands directly
in front of the car,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.650
positioning the front jack

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.170
so that it catches just a bit of momentum

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.710
from the nose cone as
the car comes to a halt.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.750
Then in once move motion,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.020
the operator lifts the car from the front.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.970
At the same time, behind the car,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.000
the rear jack operator gets into position.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.580
Since they need to leave
room for the car to enter,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.980
they're slightly slower
than the front jack,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.690
but the moment the car stops,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.653
they also slide a jack into
place lifting the back wheels.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.280
Meanwhile, on either side of the car,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.350
two stabilizers step in.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.890
Their job is to absorb the
sudden jolts from lifting,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.073
making sure it's perfectly
aligned for the stop.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.160
For changing the actual tires,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.100
the wheel change crew
consists of 12 people,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.230
three for each tire.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.550
At the center of each team

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.920
is the person holding the wheel gun.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.690
Even before the car stops,

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.770
this person is tracking its
movement aligning themselves

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.380
so they can instantly
lock onto the wheel knot

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.413
and apply torque the
moment they get a chance.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.700
Unlike regular car wheels,
which are typically fastened

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.490
with five bolts, an F1 tire is secured

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.880
with just one single lock
knot precision manufactured

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.333
with multiple poles that
the wheel gun can lock onto.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.830
The wheel guns themselves are
custom made for this purpose

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.800
and require immense strength to operate.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.240
That is because they can
spin at up to 15,000 RPM

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.090
enough force to rip it
right out of your hands

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.440
if you aren't ready for it.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:15.870
Since a couple of years,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.570
the front of each wheel
gun features an LED ring

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.870
that shows the real-time
status to the operator.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.540
The exact color coding
varies from team to team,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.650
but in our example, the gun
initially glows white indicating

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.230
that it is in loosening mode.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.210
As soon as the tire is loose

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.130
and the gunman releases the trigger,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.700
the wheel gun then
automatically switches its mode.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.310
The LED changes from white to blue,

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.920
and the gun has entered
its tightening mode,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.230
meaning the next time
the trigger is pulled,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.517
it will spin in the opposite direction.

00:03:48.517 --> 00:03:51.267
(dramatic music)

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.260
As this is happening, the
person in front of the tire

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.860
quickly moves it aside, and
in perfect synchronization,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.310
the person behind is bringing
the fresh tire into position

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.403
so that the wheel gun operator
can begin tightening it.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.760
The wheel gun automatically detects

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.740
when the wheel is fully secured

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.193
and the LED ring now lights up green.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.440
As soon as that happens,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.930
the gunman presses a button on the gun

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.150
sending an electronic confirmation

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.103
that their task is complete.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.890
While all of this is unfolding,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.620
the front jack operator
has already stepped aside

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.020
to clear the space for the car's release.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.660
The jack is designed to
keep the car lifted up

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.120
while it swivels out of place,

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.920
and once they're in a safe position,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.620
the jack operator presses
a button on the jack

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.903
to confirm they're also ready.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.430
Each of these button
presses serves one purpose,

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:52.091
to ensure that every key function
signals when they're done.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.610
(dramatic music)

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:57.240
The driver in the meantime
isn't watching the crew.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.360
They are focused on a traffic
light in front of them,

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.450
and until every single crew
member has pressed their button,

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:04.863
it will remain red.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.280
Because while the front tires are done,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.100
the rears still need to be secured.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.220
These often take slightly
longer to finish simply

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.110
because the rear jack lifts the car

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.710
a fraction of a second
later than the front jack.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.260
And on top of that, the
rear tires are larger

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.030
and heavier than the front ones,

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.073
making them a bit trickier to handle.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.820
Sometimes a driver will
have radioed in a message

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.410
that they'd like their front wing

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.540
to be adjusted during the stop.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.180
If that is the case, two mechanics step in

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.440
from the front of the car,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.860
each carrying a
pre-programmed torque driver,

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.610
they insert it into designated
holes on the front wing,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.830
and the torque driver rotates a screw

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.990
by a precise number of turns

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.390
adjusting the angle of the front wing,

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.178
which changes to car's aerodynamics.

00:05:54.178 --> 00:05:56.100
(dramatic music)

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.320
Just as these adjustments are done,

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.440
the rear tires are also secured.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.987
All four gunmen have signaled they're done

00:06:03.987 --> 00:06:07.590
and the front jack operator
has given the all clear,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.120
but for safety reasons,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.180
the light doesn't switch
to green automatically.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.330
Instead, the crew chief
who oversees everything,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.970
provides the final confirmation.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.220
Only when all has gone to plan

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.900
and when the pit lane is clear

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.210
with no other cars blocking the exit,

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.490
does the crew chief press the button.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.120
The front jack drops the
car, the light turns green,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.704
and the car takes off.

00:06:31.704 --> 00:06:34.530
(rocking music)

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.741
All that in just two and a half seconds.

00:06:37.741 --> 00:06:40.230
(gentle music)

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.570
In Formula One, clear communication

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.820
can make or break a race.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.273
And the same is also true in aviation.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.530
And what happens when
communication goes bad

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.020
is what I explore in my full length video

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.432
about the Tenerife Disaster.

00:06:57.432 --> 00:06:59.070
(gentle music)

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:00.600
The video tells the story

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.340
of how in 1977, 2 Boeing 747s collided

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.383
with each other on a runway.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:13.050
It is to this day the worst
accident in aviation history,

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.073
and it was one of my most
ambitious videos I ever worked on.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.650
This video is part of my
documentary series "Under Exposure"

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.130
a series where I bring
fascinating stories to light

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.173
that I believe deserved to be told.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:33.300
For example, I made a video
about two East German families

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.910
that fled their country with nothing

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.320
but a homemade hot air balloon
attempting the daring escape

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.440
of flying over the heavily
guarded border at night.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.170
And for this video, I
even got to interview

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:47.883
one of the people involved.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.440
The reason I was able to
make all these videos is

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.550
because I joined forces with
a bunch of other creators,

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.420
and together we are building Nebula.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.370
The idea is pretty simple.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.730
Since Nebula is not an
ad-driven platform like YouTube,

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.350
but a subscription model,

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:10.353
